# A leading-directory pattern here matches only at the root: `obj/` does NOT match
# `src/DikuWeb.Server/obj/`. Every directory that occurs at more than one depth therefore needs
# `**/`, and the two that went wrong were the expensive ones. The host's per-project `obj/` was
# being copied over the container's own restore output, so `project.assets.json` arrived naming
# Windows package paths and the publish died inside FallbackPackagePathResolver. And
# `client/node_modules` was shipping in the build context, which is most of a third of a gigabyte
# sent to the daemon on every build.

# Git
.git
.gitignore
.gitattributes

# Build artifacts
**/bin/
**/obj/
*.dll
*.pdb

# NuGet
.nuget/
*.nupkg
*.snupkg

# IDE
.vscode/
.vs/
*.swp
*.swo
*~
.DS_Store
.idea/
*.iml

# Test results
**/TestResults/
**/coverage/
*.trx

# Docker
Dockerfile
docker-compose*.yml
.dockerignore

# CI/CD
.github/
.gitlab-ci.yml
.gitea/
appveyor.yml

# Documentation
*.md
docs/
README.md
LICENSE

# ...except the canon, which is build input rather than documentation.
#
# docs/WORLD.md is embedded into the server assembly (src/DikuWeb.Server/Assist/Canon.cs) and put
# in front of every builder-assist request. Excluding it does not degrade the assist; it fails the
# compile with CS1566 "Error reading resource", which reads like a broken csproj and is really this
# file. Same reasoning as .editorconfig above: the moment a document becomes something the build
# reads, ignoring it stops being free.
!docs/WORLD.md

# Root level files
# .editorconfig is deliberately NOT ignored: Directory.Build.props sets EnforceCodeStyleInBuild,
# which makes it build input rather than an editor preference. Excluding it would compile the
# image under a different rule set from CI and from a developer's machine.
.env
.env.*.local
appsettings.local.json

# Node/npm (if using frontend build)
**/node_modules/
npm-debug.log
yarn-error.log
**/dist/

# Backups
*.bak
*.backup
backups/
